## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about small logical constraints and translate them into a checkable search, including vacuous implications and cardinality.

## Key Concepts

- Logical implications and vacuous truth
- Cardinality constraints (minimum true)
- Backtracking with pruning
- Generalizing to N variables

## Code Example

```javascript
function countTrue(arr){ return arr.filter(x=>x).length; }
function implies(p,q){ return !p || q; }
function sat(assignment){
  const [A,B,C] = assignment;
  if(!implies(A,B)) return false;
  if(!implies(B,C)) return false;
  if(countTrue(assignment) < 2) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 4+ vars with arbitrary implications?
- How would you optimize for sparse constraint graphs and avoid enumerating all 2^n assignments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft a new 3-valued operator and reason about propagation in a constraint system, a common pattern in policy engines and verification tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics
- Custom operator design (XOR3)
- Implication via ¬X ∨ Y under 3-valued logic
- Propagation and backtracking in constraint sets

## Code Example
```javascript
// 3-valued constants
const T = 'T', F = 'F', U = 'U';
function xor3(a,b){
  if(a==='U' || b==='U') return 'U';
  return a===b ? 'F' : 'T';
}
function imp(x,y){
  // Kleene: ¬x is T->F, F->T, U->U; then max with y (T>U>F)
  const not = (v)=> v==='T' ? 'F' : v==='F' ? 'T' : 'U';
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  const nx = not(x);
  return order[nx] >= order[y] ? nx : y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt XOR3 to a different ordering or to handle more than three truth values?
- How would you scale the propagation algorithm to 6–8 variables with richer constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes designing a 3-valued constraint propagator with a nontrivial rule and a forced Unknown branching, mirroring how production systems reason about partial information.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic (H>U> N) and definition of implication as ¬A ∨ B
- Constraint propagation and fixpoint computation
- Backtracking on U choices to avoid definite F outcomes

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal 3-valued logic helpers
const order = {H:2, U:1, N:0};
function not(v){ return v==='H'?'N': v==='N'?'H':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ return or(not(a), b); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
